  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Time-Scaling Property",
    "lesson_core_move": "Determine how time scaling $x(at)$ changes the fundamental period and angular frequency while reusing the original Fourier coefficient sequence with any sign-induced index reversal.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Scaling Property",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time-Scaling Property",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When a periodic signal is written as $y(t)=x(at)$, the key object to track is the scale factor $a$ acting on the original signal $x(t)$. Start from $x(t)$ with fundamental period $T_0$ and Fourier coefficients $c_k$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_y=\\frac{T_0}{|a|}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This means time compression or expansion changes the fundamental period but does not create a new coefficient sequence. The new fundamental angular frequency follows the same rescaling on the frequency grid, so a smaller period corresponds to a larger angular frequency and a larger period corresponds to a smaller angular frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_y=|a|\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $a>0$, the harmonic index $k$ keeps the same coefficient $c_k$ on the new frequency grid. The values stay in the same order; only the period and angular spacing change.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $a<0$, the sign of $a$ adds time reversal, so the coefficient at harmonic index $k$ becomes $c_{-k}$. The period and angular frequency still use $|a|$, but the index direction flips because the signal has been reversed in time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the rule to remember is: time scaling changes the period and fundamental angular frequency, while positive scaling reuses the same coefficient sequence and negative scaling reverses the harmonic indices.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Scaling Property",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Finding the New Period After Time Scaling",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)$ has fundamental period $T_0=12$, find the fundamental period of $y(t)=x(3t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a periodic signal written as $y(t)=x(at)$. Here the scaling factor is $a=3$, so the goal is to apply the period rule from the introduction rather than recompute anything from scratch.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_y=\\frac{T_0}{|a|}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the original period and the scale factor: $T_y=12/|3|$. Since $|3|=3$, the new signal is compressed in time by a factor of $3$, so its period must shrink by the same factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_y=\\frac{12}{3}=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the fundamental period of $y(t)=x(3t)$ is $4$. The result stays positive because period depends on $|a|$, even though the signal itself has been time-compressed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Scaling Property",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding the New Fundamental Angular Frequency After Time Scaling",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)$ has fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0=5$ rad/s, find the fundamental angular frequency of $y(t)=x(2t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is still the time-scaling form $y(t)=x(at)$, but now we read the frequency side of the property instead of the period side. For $y(t)=x(2t)$, the scaling factor is $a=2$ and the original fundamental angular frequency is $\\omega_0=5$ rad/s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_y=|a|\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the values: $\\omega_y=|2|(5)$. Because $|2|=2$, the frequency grid stretches by a factor of $2$ when the signal is compressed in time, so the fundamental angular frequency doubles.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_y=|2|(5)=10\\,\\text{rad/s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the new fundamental angular frequency is $10$ rad/s. This is the frequency-side counterpart of the shorter period from the previous example: time compression shrinks the period and increases the fundamental angular frequency by the same factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Scaling Property",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Reusing Coefficients for Positive Time Scaling",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Let $x(t)$ have CTFS coefficients $c_k$ with fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0$. For $y(t)=x(2t)$, identify $d_k$ and the new fundamental angular frequency.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a positive time scaling in CTFS form. For $y(t)=x(2t)$, the scaling factor is $a=2$, so we keep the original coefficient sequence but read it on a new frequency grid with doubled spacing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(2t)=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} c_k e^{jk\\omega_0(2t)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rewrite the exponent as $jk(2\\omega_0)t$. That identifies the new fundamental angular frequency as $2\\omega_0$, which is exactly what positive time compression should do to the harmonic spacing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} c_k e^{jk(2\\omega_0)t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Reading this against the standard CTFS form shows that $d_k=c_k$ for every harmonic index $k$ and $\\omega_y=2\\omega_0$. Positive scaling changes the frequency grid, not the coefficient values.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Scaling Property",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Applying Index Reversal for Negative Time Scaling",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Let $x(t)$ have coefficients $c_k$. For $y(t)=x(-2t)$, state the new fundamental angular frequency and the coefficient rule for $d_k$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same time-scaling form, but the negative sign means the frequency-grid update must be paired with time reversal. For $y(t)=x(-2t)$, separate the operation into scaling by $2$ and reversing time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(-2t)=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} c_k e^{jk\\omega_0(-2t)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $|a|$ for the frequency change, so the new fundamental angular frequency is $2\\omega_0$. The negative sign does not change the size of the frequency step; it only flips the index direction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} c_{-k} e^{jk(2\\omega_0)t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the new fundamental angular frequency is $2\\omega_0$ and the coefficient rule is $d_k=c_{-k}$. Negative scaling changes the index direction, not the coefficient values themselves.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
